    "curated_report_sections": {
      "Best result": "The v3 material need-share rate advantage replicated in seeds 404, 505, and 606. Across 64 fixed held-out worlds per seed, median POSITIVE-SHARE minus CTRL-NONE gain was `+0.9971` deterministic and `+0.7798` sampled. Median beneficial energy units per eligible need timestep gained `+2.0445` and `+2.1946`, respectively. Maximum energy-accounting error across v3 learned runs remained below `1.81e-05`.",
      "Executive summary": "Day 1 produced a tested, restartable two-agent research stack and eight immutable experiment blocks. A direct-reward v3 control learned material energy transfer on 64 held-out worlds in each of three independent training seeds. This is engineered behavior, not emergence. The same policies manufactured 5--8 times more need opportunities, exchanged energy in both directions, and shortened sampled episode length in every seed. The strict `FALSIFY-v1.1` capability gate remains closed because causal positivity/benefit is unavailable and v3 ran from an uncommitted worktree whose code is not reproduced by its recorded git revision. No JEPA outcome has been opened.",
      "Human-readable conclusion": "We can reliably train agents to make substantial transfers when directly rewarded. We cannot yet say those transfers preserve the partner: sampled behavior usually ends episodes sooner and much of the energy moves back and forth. The infrastructure has repeatedly found exactly the kinds of artifacts this sprint is meant to expose. There is still no JEPA or social-emergence result.",
      "Strongest alternative explanation": "The rewarded controller learns to approach, remain close, and cycle energy to harvest gross-transfer reward. The material-rate signal is real but need not be partner preservation. For future JEPA cells, the strongest alternative remains predictability management by immobilization, simplification, synchronization, or death.",
      "Suspicious results": "The deterministic mean action can delay first death in one seed while the sampled policy shortens life in every seed. Because every episode ends at the first death, mean alive fraction hides this difference. Fixed-horizon counterfactual survival, net receiver gain, opportunity creation, collision/contact, and role-conditioned mortality must be reported together."
    },
